      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the domain of $q(x)=2\\sqrt[4]{x+3}-2.$",
      "worked_solution": "The domain of $y=\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $q(x)=2\\sqrt[4]{x+3}-2,$ we replace $x$ with $x+3$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\nx+3\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=q(x)$ is $x\\in [-3,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=q(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14249783/assets/977b12dd8bd88d2b79285ef07d89f9958194fc84f340d7930c5b40122f8896d2.png)",
