      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the domain of $s(x)=\\sqrt[6]{2x-4}+1.$",
      "worked_solution": "The domain of $y=\\sqrt[6]{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $s(x)=\\sqrt[6]{2x-4}+1,$ we replace $x$ with $2x-4$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n2x-4\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge 4 \\\\ x & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=s(x)$ is $x\\in [2,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=s(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14249783/assets/c340fbe03170ab0dcd2ba0a7c7c32ed6712f96b0eaa944cde2dc40a232e3e32e.png)",
